{
    "title": "Airline Refunds and Other Consumer Protections",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 05, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-05/pdf/2025-22140.pdf",
    "summary": "Two federal updates aim to fine-tune costs for small business investment funds and clarify when airlines owe refunds after a flight number change. One raises the minimum annual charges on certain government-backed investment loans over the next three years. The other temporarily pauses enforcement of refunds when only the flight number changes and the trip itself stays the same.\n\nFirst, the Small Business Administration updated how it charges Small Business Investment Companies that use government-backed loans called debentures. The annual charge can still be set as high as 1.38% a year, but the minimum floor will step up over time: at least 0.30% for commitments approved on or after October 1, 2026, at least 0.35% on or after October 1, 2027, and at least 0.40% on or after October 1, 2028. The agency may set different rates for different types of debentures. It issued this as a direct final rule, saying the change is narrow and not expected to materially affect a large number of small entities.\n\nWho stands to benefit here are the long-term finances and predictability of the investment program, which can support stability for taxpayers and investors. Those who may feel a pinch are the investment funds that use this leverage, which could face slightly higher costs over time and may pass some of that along to portfolio companies.\n\nSecond, the Department of Transportation announced it will pause until June 30, 2026 the rule that treats any flight number change as a cancellation that triggers a refund. During this period, if an airline changes the flight number but keeps your itinerary essentially the same\u2014no major shift in departure or arrival time, no change of airports, no extra stops, no downgrade in service, and no loss of needed accessibility features\u2014you\u2019ll be rebooked but not automatically eligible for a refund based solely on the number change. The department plans to propose a new rule in early 2026 to clarify what counts as a cancellation, and all other consumer protections remain in place.\n\nAirlines and their customers may benefit from fewer \u201cpaper cancellations\u201d tied only to number changes, especially during schedule integrations or mergers. Passengers who would have preferred a refund after a number change alone may be negatively affected during this pause. If your trip meaningfully changes or is delayed, your refund rights still apply.",
